my site [sccl.cc](https://sccl.cc) was originally on [astro](https://astro.build) (actually ORIGINALLY, it was just on nodejs, back in the distant covid days)

astro is a cool thing - not bad for quickly knocking together something pretty, but over time i kinda stopped liking it, feels heavy and stuff.

i came across [zine](https://zine-ssg.io) - an ssg in zig. - you can build a site with just one binary without any dependencies.

templates are in superhtml - that's valid html with templating added through attributes like `:text`, `:if`, `:loop`.

Content is in supermd - like .md, but supposedly cooler.

# # the moving process
overall moving is pretty simple - gotta rewrite layouts from astro to superhtml, move over the existing scripts. the most tedious part is that astro uses its own fucking `.astro` files with their weird mix of js and html - had to change the approach a bit.

*upd: the approach changed a few more times later, with the arrival of my own crutches :sob:*

# # deploy
zine is compiled into a statically linked standalone binary.

you can build through npm in ci, as the wiki recommends - using `kristoff-it/setup-zine@v1`:

like this:
```yaml
- uses: kristoff-it/setup-zine@v1
  with:
    version: v0.11.3
- run: zine release
```

my site used to be on cf - pushed with wrangler like this:
```yaml
- uses: cloudflare/wrangler-action@v3
  with:
    apiToken: ${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN }}
    accountId: ${{ secrets.CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID }}
    command: pages deploy public/ -project-name sccl-cc
```
*now fully self-hosted it's a bit simpler*

# ? goodies

the coolest thing about zine is that its build is mega-fast, it generally doesn't accumulate a hundred dependencies like js frameworks do, with a giant `node_modules` and so on..

quite a lot of control, which some frameworks piss away with their abstraction levels - for my page weight minimalism that's a critical plus.

# ? downsides

zine is beta :partying_face: (v0.11.0 at the time of writing)

honestly i don't see any real downsides yet - just caveats from the fact that it's not some framework or whatever, but a static site generator meant for making microblogs and such,
though i'm trying to bolt on js and apis too - and i seem to like it all, everything works, but in places i have to hack together some custom stuff, cuz it's not zine-native, and i need it.
